      "title": "Graduation Specialist",
      "description": "Department: UM Student Records JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Orono, ME, United States The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. Supports OSR operations related to review and certification of academic requirements, with a primary focus on graduation eligibility and related processes. Key duties include processing of exceptions and waivers, auditing and certification of students’ eligibility for graduation, and seeing graduation applications through to their final resolution. Oversees processes related to diplomas. Typical hiring salary for this position is $47,500.00 commensurate with qualifications and experience. Please review the Graduation Specialist job description for more information. About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Requried: Bachelor’s degree. Experience in handling sensitive and confidential information. Strong attention to detail. Ability to analyze and leverage data effectively. Excellent interpersonal skills, ability to function as a team member. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated exemplary customer service. Dependable, self-motivated, organized, and possess the ability to problem solve. Ability to follow established policies and procedures. Ability to work independently, and with minimal supervision. Preferred: Prior experience at an institution of higher education in student records or related areas. Experience with automated student information systems preferred, Oracle PeopleSoft highly. Experience with certifying students’ eligibility for graduation and/or athletic eligibility. Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on October 30, 2026. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3213": {
      "title": "Scientific Research Specialist CL2",
      "description": "Department: UM Administration NFA JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Jonesboro, ME, United States The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. The individual in this position provides scientific and field research technical support to the University of Maine’s wild blueberry research program, based at Blueberry Hill Farm (BHF) in Jonesboro with occasional duties at the Wyman’s Center for Wild Blueberry Research and Innovation in Old Town. The position supports multiple faculty researchers working on wild blueberry entomology, plant pathology, integrated pest management (IPM), crop management, and irrigation/sustainability, and assists with federally funded minor-crop pesticide research conducted under the IR-4 program. The incumbent establishes and maintains research plots, scouts and samples for insect pests and diseases, collects and manages field research data, and assists with irrigation and water-use studies. The position also assists the Farm Superintendent with general farm operations and equipment needs, including operating tractors and other equipment during planting, pruning, and harvest, ensuring the farm has reliable, cross-trained support across research and production activities. Full-time, regular position, 40 hours per week, 5 days per week. Normal daily work hours are Monday – Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. This position pays $21.93/hour, or applicable transfer rate in accordance with the ACSUM collective bargaining agreement. Essential duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Assists faculty researchers (e.g., in plant pathology, entomology, agroecology, IPM/crop management, weed science, and irrigation/sustainability) with establishing, maintaining, and harvesting wild blueberry field research plots at Blueberry Hill Farm in Jonesboro and occasionally at the Wyman’s Center in Old Town. Conducts field scouting and sample collection for insect pests (e.g., blueberry maggot, spotted wing drosophila) and diseases (e.g., mummy berry, stem and leaf diseases; and Collects weekly in-season crop phenology data. Assists with IPM research, including deployment and monitoring of insect traps, disease incidence surveys, and collection of field-plot data. Supports irrigation and water-use research, including installing, operating, and maintaining irrigation equipment and soil moisture monitoring instrumentation. Assists with IR-4 minor-crop pesticide research, including preparing and applying experimental treatments, maintaining required Maine Commercial Pesticide Applicators License, maintaining application and safety records (e.g., FIFRA Good Laboratory Practice Standards) and assisting with residue/tolerance sample collection in support of EPA registration petitions. Contacts vendors and suppliers to order research and farm supplies and assists faculty with sourcing materials for field trials. Enter, organize, and maintain research data (yield, pest and disease counts, weather, irrigation) in spreadsheets/databases for faculty use, and supports student research assistants and seasonal field staff on data collection protocols and the safe operation of field equipment. Prepares, analyzes and ships samples for laboratory analysis. Assists in the preparation of scientific reports and manuscripts, as well as research-related outreach publications and presentations. Assists the Farm Superintendent with general farm and mechanical support, including routine equipment upkeep, and operates farm tractors and other equipment during peak management, pruning, and harvest periods. Coordinate and supervise yearly soil sampling at Blueberry Hill Farm and other MAFES Maintains and updates logs, checklists, work records and safety documentation Adheres to all safety protocols and assists with maintaining compliance in general areas. Performs other duties of a similar nature or level at MAFES facilities, in coordination with the MAFES associate director and superintendents, as needed. About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Required: Bachelor’s degree in a field related to the area of research or equivalent and more than 2 years of related laboratory research experience. Additional qualifying experience may substitute for the education required of the assigned position. Demonstrated ability to accurately collect, record, and organize field research data. Demonstrated ability to solve problems and overcome challenges. Communication, interpersonal skills as applied to interaction with coworkers, supervisors, the public, etc., sufficient to exchange or convey information and to receive work direction. Ability to learn and correctly apply field research and data collection protocols under the direction of faculty researchers, and to identify or learn to identify common wild blueberry insect pests, diseases, and plant growth stages. Possesses a valid Maine Class B driver’s license or ability to obtain one within 60 days of employment. Possesses a Maine pesticide applicators license or ability to obtain one within 60 days of employment Ability to work outdoors in a variety of conditions, including heat, cold, dust, mud, and snow. Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. Preferred: Higher education degree (MS, MA, PhD) in agricultural sciences or a related field. Previous experience with pesticide application and IR-4 trials. 1-3 years of experience working in commercial agricultural settings and/or agricultural research trials, preferably with small-fruit crops. Previous experience with agricultural irrigation systems (pivot, lateral line, drip). Experience with basic carpentry and maintenance of power tools and equipment. Demonstrated skills with data management and statistical analysis (e.g., Google & Microsoft Office Suites, R, MATLAB, ArcGIS) Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on October 30, 2026. For questions about the search, please contact search committee chair Lee Hecker at lee.hecker@maine.edu. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening and post offer physical. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3217": {
      "title": "Lead Equipment Operator",
      "description": "Department: UM Admin Physical Plant JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Orono, ME, United States The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. This classification performs defined responsibilities related to the operation of vehicles and/or heavy equipment of various sizes and complexity, and on various levels of projects. Incumbents may be assigned to operate a single or multiple types of heavy equipment and may work on defined tasks or multiple phases of a project. This classification may set up the worksite and/or leads a crew based on the campus and project assignment. Full-time, regular position, 40 hours per week, 5 days per week. Normal daily work hours are Monday – Friday, 7:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. This position pays $26.64/hour. Essential duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Trailer excavator to job sites Snow removal Landscaping and lawnmowing Event Set up and tear down Line striping Office moves Asphalt patching General Earth work Write Estimates Demolition Manual Labor Maintaining Catch Basins Oversee maintenance needs of equipment, log maintenance and fleet needs by reviewing logs to ensure accuracy, requesting equipment needs, and scheduling maintenance Read and interpret blueprints for site work and safety compliance, ensure Federal and State regulations are implemented Proper setting up work site Inspecting work of other Equipment Operators to ensure they can proceed Inspecting and maintaining their equipment; related duties in support of other departments Provide work direction and review to tea of Equipment Operators Provides dig safe instructions on site, directing crew, determines flow of work, safety follow-ups, inspect work of others Performs other duties of a similar nature or level About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Required: High school diploma or equivalent (G.E.D) (Class A) Commercial Driver’s License Five years of heavy equipment operation experience Exposure to OSHA (State and Federal), NIOSH, DOT confined space regulations Trailering excavator experience Skills: Customer service principles Procedures for operating, inspecting, maintaining, and storing assigned equipment Traffic laws and regulations governing equipment operation Occupational hazards involved and the safety precautions necessary to the proper operation of assigned motorized equipment; use of assigned tools/equipment Procedures and techniques involved in assigned work processes Ability to read and interpret blueprints Occupational requirements per OSHA, DOT, etc. Identifying equipment malfunctions Maintaining records and reports, if required Providing training to other employees, if required Performing heavy manual labor for extended periods of time, often under unfavorable conditions Positions in this class typically require: climbing, balancing, stooping, kneeling, crouching, crawling, reaching, standing, walking, pushing, pulling, lifting, grasping, fingering, feeling, talking, hearing, seeing and repetitive motions. Heavy work: The ability to lift up to 50 pounds frequently, and/or in excess of 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Lifting and exerting forces more than 50 pounds requires assistance by other employee(s) or mechanical assistance. Individual forces must never exceed 50 pounds. Incumbents may be subjected to moving mechanical parts, vibrations, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, poor ventilation, wetness, humidity, fire, chemicals, oils, extreme temperatures, difficult driving conditions, inadequate lighting, workspace restrictions, intense noises and travel. Ability to conform to Federal DOT drug and alcohol testing rules and regulations. Ability to work long hours as needed (snow removal and emergency situations) as directed. Other Information: Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on October 19, 2026. For questions about the search, please contact search committee chair Jodi Munster at jodi.munster@maine.edu or 581-2720. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening and post offer physical. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
