    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3089": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Extension Professor, PFAS and Environmental Contaminants Analytical Specialist, and PFAS Lab Director",
      "description": "Department: UM Cooperative Extension JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Orono, ME, United States The University of Maine Cooperative Extension is seeking an accomplished scientist and collaborative leader to establish and grow the University's PFAS analytical and Extension program. This is a unique opportunity to build research capacity, direct a state-of-the-art analytical laboratory, and work alongside agricultural producers, researchers, and policymakers to address the evolving challenges of PFAS contamination in Maine. The successful candidate will combine scientific excellence with a commitment to public engagement, using applied research and Extension education to translate complex environmental science into practical solutions that strengthen Maine's agricultural and food systems. Typical hiring range for this position is $85,000 to $90,000 commensurate with qualifications and experience. Complete Job Description Who We Are: The University of Maine Cooperative Extension has been a vital resource for over 100 years, connecting communities with university research to improve lives across the state. Our focus areas include the Maine Food System and Positive Youth Development through 4-H programs, delivered by a network of experts based in county offices statewide. We pride ourselves on being an equitable, sustainable, and enjoyable workplace, and we welcome diverse perspectives and experiences. We also believe in work and life balance and offer benefits to support you. Learn more about our mission and work at extension.umaine.edu . The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu. The University of Maine System provides a highly competitive benefits package that includes: holidays, vacation and sick time; health, dental and vision insurance; life insurance; short and long term disability insurance; a tuition waiver program for employee and eligible dependents; a 403(b) retirement plan; and more. Knowledge and Skill Qualifications We Are Looking For : Required: These are required knowledge and skills so be sure that you demonstrate how you meet each one in your resume or cover letter. Doctorate degree in the area of, Analytical Chemistry, Environmental Chemistry, Chemistry, Soil Science, Environmental Science, or a closely related field, by date of hire. Approximately 5 years of experience in PFAS analysis, pesticide residue analysis, or similar work. Demonstrated experience with LC-MS/MS analysis, data interpretation, troubleshooting, instrument maintenance, and vendor/service coordination. Demonstrated experience managing annual laboratory budgets, fiscal-year planning, recharge/service-center operations, procurement, service contracts, cost recovery, and financial reporting. Documented record of conducting high-quality, applied scientific research, demonstrated by publication in peer-reviewed journals and/or popular press. Experience supervising, training, and evaluating technical staff, research staff, students, postdoctoral researchers, and/or laboratory analysts. Experience in writing and maintaining standard operating procedures Experience analyzing PFAS or trace organic contaminants in environmental and/or agricultural matrices such as drinking water, groundwater, wastewater, soil, biosolids, sediment, plant tissue, forage, food crops, animal tissue, or related samples. Knowledge of current and emerging issues, practices, and trends in the PFAS analytical methods field. Demonstrated experience in managing the financial aspects of a fee-for-service lab and creating a sustainable annual budget. Demonstrated computer and educational technology skills including creating and maintaining analytical database systems. Effective verbal and written communication skills with the ability to articulate clear goals. Demonstrated experience with applied research and community needs assessments. Excellent interpersonal, technical writing, and public communication skills. Ability to creatively solve problems and manage conflicts within groups. Preferred: These are preferred knowledge and skills we are looking for but do not exclude you from the job if you don’t have this experience. Experience working in an accredited, regulated, service-center, or compliance-driven analytical laboratory. Experience with laboratory information management systems, sample tracking software, electronic data deliverables, or analytical data management platforms. Experience with automated SPE systems, automated extraction systems, LC-MS/MS troubleshooting, instrument maintenance, and vendor/service coordination. Experience communicating PFAS analytical results to non-specialist audiences, including farmers, agency partners, community members, and decision makers. Experience building partnerships with state agencies, federal agencies, agricultural organizations, commodity groups, or affected communities. Experience with Cooperative Extension educational program development, delivery and evaluation. Experience in grant writing and the financial management of grant funds. Experience working with diverse audiences; knowledge of diversity, equity and inclusion; and understanding of equal opportunity, affirmative action and civil rights policies. Successful supervisory experience. Proficiency with computer programs such as Microsoft Excel, Word and PowerPoint, as well as Google Drive and document management software. Benefits: Benefits are an important part of your total compensation. The University of Maine System (UMS) strives to provide high-quality, affordable, family-friendly benefit options to meet the needs of our diverse workforce. We offer an excellent benefits package that includes paid time off (holidays, vacation and sick), health, dental, vision and life insurance, short- and long-term disability insurance, a tuition waiver program for eligible employees and dependents, a 403(b) retirement plan and more. Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 pm on October 11th, 2026. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. In complying with the letter and spirit of applicable laws and pursuing its own goals of diversity, the University of Maine System does not discriminate on the grounds of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability physical or mental, genetic information, or veterans or military status in employment, education, and all other programs and activities. The University provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding non-discrimination policies: Director of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services, 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5713, 207.581.1226, TTY 711 (Maine Relay System). Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report. If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3126": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Nursing",
      "description": "Department: UM School of Nursing JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Orono, ME, United States The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. The School of Nursing at the University of Maine seeks applicants for a full-time, academic-year, tenure-track, Associate/Full Professor position. The initial appointment for this position will be to serve as the PI on an existing Department of Education Grant to launch New England’s first PhD program in Nursing. The successful candidate will provide academic leadership for the development and implementation of the program. The faculty member will ensure the quality and alignment of the PhD curriculum, support faculty teaching within the program, recruit and mentor doctoral students, and develop partnerships with healthcare organizations and institutions of higher education. The position will also advance the School of Nursing’s research mission through an active program of scholarship, grant development, research dissemination, and doctoral-student mentorship. The faculty member will teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned and contribute service to the School, University, profession, and public. At the conclusion of the DOE grant, the teaching, research, and administrative responsibilities will be negotiated based on ongoing need. Typical hiring range for this position is $80,000 to $95,000 commensurate with qualifications and experience. PhD program leadership throughout the funded Department of Education grant In collaboration with the Associate Director of Graduate Programs and the SON Director: Provide academic leadership for Northern New England’s first PhD program in Nursing. Guide the implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the PhD program. Ensure that courses and curricula align with program outcomes, University requirements, and applicable professional and accreditation standards. Facilitate collaboration among PhD faculty to promote cohesive, rigorous, and high-quality course design and instruction. Coordinate the program’s required immersion experiences. Recruit prospective PhD students and support activities designed to promote enrollment and retention. Meet with prospective students and provide information about the program, admissions process, curriculum, and career pathways. Develop partnerships with healthcare organizations, academic institutions, and other stakeholders to promote the PhD program and expand opportunities for collaboration. Seek internal and external funding to support PhD students, research, and program development. Ongoing responsibilities Teaching and student mentorship Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned. Develop and deliver high-quality, evidence-informed instruction across appropriate teaching modalities. Advise and mentor doctoral students throughout their programs of study. Mentor students in research development, scholarly writing, dissemination, and grant preparation. Chair or serve on dissertation committees, as assigned. Participate in the assessment of student learning and ongoing evaluation of courses and curricula. Preference will be given to candidates qualified to teach: Measurement in Health and Behavioral Science Research Methods PhD Dissertation Seminar Dissertation Research Research and scholarship Maintain an active and sustained research agenda within the faculty member’s area of expertise. Pursue internal and external funding to support research, doctoral education, and student scholarship. Disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications and presentations at regional, national, and international conferences. Collaborate with faculty, students, healthcare organizations, and other partners on research and scholarly initiatives. Engage doctoral students in research, grant development, publication, and scholarly dissemination. Service Serve on School of Nursing and University committees. Contribute service to the nursing profession, healthcare community, and public. Participate in student recruitment, program events, commencement, and other School and University activities. Support the School’s academic, research, accreditation, and strategic priorities. Work location and travel expectations The successful candidate may reside anywhere in Maine. The position requires regular remote engagement and attendance at designated on-campus activities, including commencement, PhD immersion experiences, and other required meetings or program events. Travel throughout Maine and, when necessary, the broader region will be required for student recruitment, partnership development, and meetings with prospective students, healthcare organizations, and institutions of higher education. University-approved workspace will be provided near the employee’s primary residence, subject to institutional policies and availability. Faculty workload This is a tenure-track faculty appointment with workload allocated among teaching, research, and service. At the conclusion of the DOE grant in 2027, the teaching, research, and administrative responsibilities will be negotiated based on ongoing need. A full teaching load is equivalent to 12 teaching credits per semester. Teaching release for approved program leadership, research, clinical practice, or service responsibilities is determined by the school director in accordance with institutional policies and available resources. About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Required: Earned PhD in Nursing from an accredited institution. Active, unrestricted registered nurse license in the State of Maine. Record of peer-reviewed scholarly publications and professional presentations commensurate with appointment at the rank of associate professor or professor. Demonstrated record of developing and sustaining an independent program of research following completion of the PhD. Experience mentoring PhD students through the dissertation process, including research development, proposal preparation, study implementation, scholarly writing, and dissertation completion. Demonstrated success pursuing or securing internal or external research funding. Knowledge of doctoral nursing education, curriculum development, program evaluation, and continuous quality improvement. Evidence of effective academic leadership and collaboration with faculty, students, healthcare organizations, and institutional partners. Strong written, interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills. Commitment to excellence in teaching, research, student mentorship, and academic service. Preferred: Experience leading or developing a PhD program in nursing. Experience chairing PhD dissertation committees. Record of externally funded research. Experience developing partnerships with healthcare organizations and institutions of higher education. Expertise teaching one or more of the following: Measurement in Health and Behavioral Science Research Methods PhD Dissertation Seminar Dissertation Research Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on October 12, 2026. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3139": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Twelve Positions",
      "description": "Department: UM School of Nursing JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Orono, ME, United States The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. Essential duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: 12 part-time faculty positions for multiple course designers for the PhD program. Seeking faculty with experience and expertise in designing the following courses: Research Practicum I (3 credits) Measurement in Health & Behavior Sciences (3 credits) Conceptual Models & Frameworks in Nursing Science (3 credits) Research Practicum II (3 credits) Introduction to Scholarly Writing (1 credit) NUR 699 Dissertation Research (3 credits) NUR 561 Population Health (3 credits) The University of Maine School of Nursing is seeking applicants for up to 12 part-time faculty positions to support the design and development of courses for its synchronous PhD in Nursing program. Applicants must hold a Ph.D and MSN and have relevant teaching and professional expertise in one or more of the following areas: Research Practicum I Measurement in Health and Behavioral Sciences Conceptual Models and Frameworks in Nursing Science Research Practicum II Introduction to Scholarly Writing and Dissertation Research Successful candidates will have demonstrated experience in designing and teaching high-quality online courses and in conducting research. Faculty will collaborate with the University’s Center for Innovation in Teaching and Learning and receive support from graduate nursing students throughout the development process. All courses will follow a consistent program template, be designed according to evidence-based practices for online graduate education, and use current communication and collaboration technologies established at the university. To view the Credit Hour Rates, please refer to the PATFA Contract . About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Required: Ph.D and MSN Relevant teaching and professional expertise in one or more of the following areas: Research Practicum I Measurement in Health and Behavioral Sciences Conceptual Models and Frameworks in Nursing Science Research Practicum II Introduction to Scholarly Writing and Dissertation Research Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on September 28, 2026. For questions about the search, please contact search committee chair Kelley at kelley.strout@maine.edu The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. "
